#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111621} :activity/id #uuid "0c2ae7ff-1f23-5301-bf8e-7e77e5e396c2" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111813 :tutorial/content "Recall that Prim's algorithm finds a minimum spanning tree of a connected, weighted graph $G$. It follows a so-called greedy approach by starting from an arbitrary vertex and expanding the tree by adding the smallest edge that connects a visited vertex to an unvisited vertex.\n\nOne advantage of Prim's algorithm over other spanning-tree algorithms is that it can easily be applied to a distance matrix. The steps of Prim's algorithm applied to a distance matrix are as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Begin by marking the column corresponding to the starting vertex.\n- **Step 2:** Cancel out the row corresponding to the starting vertex.\n- **Step 3:** While not all columns are marked, find the smallest distance $s$ among all the marked columns. Identify the corresponding row and column, denoted as $u$ and $v$. Then, mark the column $u$ to indicate that the vertex has been added to the tree. Cancel out the row $u$ corresponding to $s$ to prevent revisiting, and add the edge $v-u$ to the tree.\n- **Step 4:** Repeat this process until all vertices are included in the minimum spanning tree, ensuring that the tree grows by selecting the smallest available edge at each step.\n\nThe pseudocode for Prim's algorithm on a distance matrix is the following:\n\n> `Mark the column corresponding to the starting vertex`\n> `Cancel out the row corresponding to the starting vertex`\n> `while (not all columns are marked):`\n> `Find the smallest distance s over all the marked columns`\n> `Let u and v be the row and column corresponding to s`\n> `Mark the column u corresponding to s`\n> `Cancel out the row u corresponding to s`\n> `Add edge v-u to the tree`\n\nIt's easiest to understand the algorithm by following a concrete example, so let's do that." :tutorial/id #uuid "6242e2b5-5d12-56eb-bf74-65cd91f84217" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10551 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "869a603d-d323-5f47-a0e9-3667315aa0b7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111622}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111816 :tutorial/content "|  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ | $v_5$ |\n| $v_1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $8$ | $26$ | $-$ |\n| $v_2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $13$ | $19$ | $3$ |\n| $v_3$ | $8$ | $13$ | $-$ | $11$ | $-$ |\n| $v_4$ | $26$ | $19$ | $11$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $v_5$ | $-$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nLet's use Prim's algorithm to find the minimum spanning tree of the weighted graph G with the distance matrix given above.\n\nWe start from the vertex $v_1$, and proceed as follows:\n\n| **Step 1.** Mark column $v_1$ by placing a \"$1$\" above it, and cancel out row $v_1$. |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$-$$-$$13$$19$$3$$v_3$$8$$13$$-$$11$$-$$v_4$$26$$19$$11$$-$$-$$v_5$$-$$3$$-$$-$$-$ | Added edgesLength |\n| **Step 2.** The smallest unselected value in the marked column is $8$, located at the intersection of row $v_3$ and column $v_1$. So, we mark column $v_3$ by placing a \"$2$\" above it, cancel out row $v_3$, and add edge $v_1-v_3$ to the tree. |  |\n| $1$$2$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$-$$-$$13$$19$$3$$v_3$$8$$\\color{lightgrey}13$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_4$$26$$19$$11$$-$$-$$v_5$$-$$3$$-$$-$$-$ | Added edgesLength$v_1 - v_3$$8$ |\n| **Step 3.** The smallest unselected value in the marked columns is $11$, located at the intersection of row $v_4$ and column $v_3$. So, we mark column $v_4$, cancel out row $v_4$, and add edge $v_3-v_4$ to the tree. |  |\n| $1$$2$$3$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$-$$-$$13$$19$$3$$v_3$$8$$\\color{lightgrey}13$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_4$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}19$$11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_5$$-$$3$$-$$-$$-$ | Added edgesLength$v_1 - v_3$$8$$v_3 - v_4$$11$ |\n| **Step 4.** The smallest unselected value in the marked columns is $13$, located at the intersection of row $v_2$ and column $v_3$. So, we mark column $v_2$, cancel out row $v_2$, and add edge $v_3-v_2$ to the tree. |  |\n| $1$$4$$2$$3$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$13$$\\color{lightgrey}19$$\\color{lightgrey}3$$v_3$$8$$\\color{lightgrey}13$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_4$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}19$$11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_5$$-$$3$$-$$-$$-$ | Added edgesLength$v_1 - v_3$$8$$v_3 - v_4$$11$$v_3 - v_2$$13$ |\n| **Step 5.** The smallest unselected value in the marked columns is $3$, located at the intersection of row $v_5$ and column $v_2$. So, we mark column $v_5$, cancel out row $v_5$, and add edge $v_2-v_5$ to the tree. |  |\n| $1$$4$$2$$3$$5$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$13$$\\color{lightgrey}19$$\\color{lightgrey}3$$v_3$$8$$\\color{lightgrey}13$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_4$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}19$$11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_5$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$3$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$ | Added edgesLength$v_1 - v_3$$8$$v_3 - v_4$$11$$v_3 - v_2$$13$$v_2 - v_5$$3$ |\n\nEvery vertex is now in our tree (every column has been marked), so the process is completed. The list of edges in our minimum spanning tree is given by the table on the right, and the total weight of the resulting minimum spanning tree is\n\n$$\n8 + 11 + 13 + 3 = 35\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "89cbe76e-6489-51ba-b816-3b4ce44c4010" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11617 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"} :step/id #uuid "3fcb5de9-c19a-50d2-9ee8-a7e7da769b2f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110680 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "601ee73c-29a0-5636-8f33-e32efa4c1ded"} :step/id #uuid "c7acea05-0815-506c-a292-1205377d227e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110681 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "18104f56-26c0-5538-8e37-8aee8de0fd36"} :step/id #uuid "09b128ab-11d0-5abb-b1c0-514a9adfa0e8"}] :activity/title "Applying Prim's Algorithm to a Distance Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110812}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048288 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110680 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110366 :question/id #uuid "f1194521-e45f-5f3c-84f6-5994748602ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-16370" :question/problem "|  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ | $v_5$ |\n| $v_1$ | $-$ | $1$ | $-$ | $3$ | $-$ |\n| $v_2$ | $1$ | $-$ | $5$ | $-$ | $7$ |\n| $v_3$ | $-$ | $5$ | $-$ | $9$ | $-$ |\n| $v_4$ | $3$ | $-$ | $9$ | $-$ | $11$ |\n| $v_5$ | $-$ | $7$ | $-$ | $11$ | $-$ |\n\nConsider the distance matrix of a weighted graph shown above.\n\nPrim's algorithm for finding a minimum spanning tree is applied to the matrix, starting at the vertex $v_1$, and after a few steps, we arrive at the configuration shown below.\nWhich edge will be added to the tree at the next step of the algorithm?\n\n| $1$$2$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}3$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}5$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}7$$v_3$$-$$5$$-$$9$$-$$v_4$$3$$-$$9$$-$$11$$v_5$$-$$7$$-$$11$$-$ | Added edgesLength$v_1 - v_2$$1$ |\n| --- | ---: |" :question/worked-solution "The pseudocode for Prim's algorithm on a distance matrix is the following:\n\n> `Mark the column corresponding to the starting vertex`\n> `Cancel out the row corresponding to the starting vertex`\n> `while (not all columns are marked):`\n> `Find the smallest distance over all the marked columns`\n> `Let u and v be the row and column corresponding to s`\n> `Mark the column u corresponding to s`\n> `Cancel out the row u corresponding to s`\n> `Add edge v-u to the tree`\n\nSo, at the next step of Prim's algorithm, we consider the smallest unselected distance over all marked columns ($v_1$, and $v_2$). The smallest value is $3$ at the intersection of row $v_4$ and column $v_1$.\n\nWe cancel out the row $v_4$ and add $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}v_1-v_4}$ of length $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}$ to the tree.\n\n|  | $1$ | $2$ |  | $3$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ | $v_5$ |\n| $v_1$ | $\\color{lightgrey}-$ | $\\color{lightgrey}1$ | $\\color{lightgrey}-$ | $\\color{lightgrey}3$ | $\\color{lightgrey}-$ |\n| $v_2$ | $1$ | $\\color{lightgrey}-$ | $\\color{lightgrey}5$ | $\\color{lightgrey}-$ | $\\color{lightgrey}7$ |\n| $v_3$ | $-$ | $5$ | $-$ | $9$ | $-$ |\n| $v_4$ | $3$ | $\\color{lightgrey}-$ | $\\color{lightgrey}9$ | $\\color{lightgrey}-$ | $\\color{lightgrey}11$ |\n| $v_5$ | $-$ | $7$ | $-$ | $11$ | $-$ |"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "601ee73c-29a0-5636-8f33-e32efa4c1ded" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "992271f8-d4b3-5df1-